<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Animals in Our Waters – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Animals in Our Waters</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Outline the main information from simple diagrams (e.g., graphs, bar charts). Reading: Comprehend descriptive texts about places or events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can outline the main points from bar charts about marine biodiversity in Panama by identifying key species and trends (e.g., "List the two most common species found in Coiba National Park's coral reefs." "Summarize the percentage of mangrove coverage shown in the chart for the Gulf of Montijo." "Identify changes in fish populations in the Bay of Panama over time based on the bar chart.")
Can comprehend descriptive texts about ocean ecosystems in Panama by identifying key characteristics and ecological importance (e.g., "Describe the coral formations and marine species found in Isla Iguanas reef." "Explain why the Bocas del Toro archipelago is known for its seagrass beds and dolphin populations." "List the unique species that thrive in the Coiba National Park marine reserve.")
Can outline trends from graphs about ocean pollution levels (e.g., "The graph shows that plastic waste in the ocean has increased over the past five years.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. This stage activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points to Panama. Then, shows pictures of various Panamanian marine animals (e.g., dolphins, sea turtles, specific fish species, coral formations) and environments (e.g., mangroves, coral reefs, coastal areas). Students identify the animals and briefly share anything they remember about them from the previous lesson, using vocabulary such as "ocean," "marine life," "fish," and "coral reefs." The teacher asks, "What do these animals need to survive?" to link to habitat and conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a short text or diagram title for the main topic. For example, the teacher shows a simplified bar chart title like "Fish Species in Coiba National Park" and says, "This title tells me the chart is about *fish species* in *Coiba National Park*. I don't need to read all the numbers yet, just the main idea." The teacher also models reading aloud key vocabulary words like "ecosystem" and "biodiversity," emphasizing stress and checking pronunciation of /iː/ in "reef" and /ɪ/ in "riff."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text describes a 'diverse ecosystem,' does that mean it has only one type of animal or many different types?" (Expected: Many different types.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a chart shows 'pollution levels,' is that a good thing or a bad thing for marine life?" (Expected: Bad thing.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher introduces a short informational text and a bar chart about marine life in Panama.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "Panama's Marine Treasures: Coiba National Park" (a descriptive text excerpt about its coral reefs, biodiversity, and protected status) and a simple bar chart titled "Key Marine Species in Coiba National Park" showing the relative abundance of 3-4 species (e.g., "parrotfish," "reef sharks," "sea turtles"). The text includes grammar: present simple for descriptions ("Coiba *is* a vast marine reserve," "There *are* many species"), and vocabulary: "ocean," "coral reefs," "marine life," "ecosystem," "biodiversity," "species," "protected zones."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading for specific details and overall comprehension; outlining main information from a bar chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students first skim the text and bar chart quickly.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main topic of the text and the chart?" (Expected: Marine life/conservation in Coiba National Park.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements (Text):</b> Students read the text and decide if statements are True or False (e.g., "Coiba National Park is a small island." "Many different species live in Coiba's coral reefs.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Chart):</b> Students match images of species to their corresponding bars on the chart, or answer simple questions like, "Which species has the highest number according to the chart?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Students practice extracting and outlining information using target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice outlining information from texts and diagrams, comprehending descriptive texts, and identifying key trends. They use Present simple for descriptions and facts, and modals for suggestions (implied from reading about conservation issues). Vocabulary: "ocean," "animals," "species," "coral reefs," "marine life," "ecosystem," "biodiversity," "conservation," "protected zones."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with a short summary of the "Panama's Marine Treasures" text, but with 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., "biodiversity," "habitat," "protect," "endangered") removed. Students re-read the original text to fill in the blanks, ensuring correct word usage and comprehension of the text's meaning.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Diagram Outline:</b> Students work individually to answer specific questions based on the "Key Marine Species in Coiba National Park" bar chart. Examples: "List the two most common species found in Coiba National Park's coral reefs." "What does the chart tell us about the number of reef sharks compared to parrotfish?" "Identify one trend shown in the chart." This activity directly targets the outlining of main information from simple diagrams.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Summarize and Suggest:</b> In pairs, students take turns. One student reads a new short paragraph about a different aspect of ocean life (e.g., "Mangroves in the Gulf of Montijo" or "Ocean Pollution trends") or describes a new simple graph, and the other student summarizes the main points using their own words. They then discuss what actions "we should" take based on the information, practicing modals for suggestions and conservation actions: "We should stop littering near oceans to protect marine animals because the text says plastic pollution is harmful."</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Marine Ecosystem Fact Sheet Creation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with a different short descriptive text and/or a simple graph/bar chart about another specific Panamanian marine ecosystem (e.g., Isla Iguana, Bocas del Toro seagrass beds) or a specific conservation challenge (e.g., impact of plastic waste, endangered species trends). The texts are slightly more complex than in Stage 2 but still at A2.4 level. Groups' task is to read their assigned materials, identify the key characteristics, important species, and any conservation needs or challenges outlined. They will then collaboratively create a "Fact Sheet" by outlining the main information from their texts/diagrams on a poster board or large paper, using bullet points, short descriptions, and labeled visuals (simple drawings or printed images). They must use the target vocabulary (e.g., "habitat," "endangered," "preserve," "pollution") and grammar (e.g., "This area *is* known for...", "There *are* many unique species...", "We *should* protect...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in extracting information from reading materials, collaborative interaction to synthesize information, and communicative task completion by producing an informative fact sheet based on their reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher circulates during Stage 4, observing each group's collaborative reading process and the quality of their "Fact Sheet" content. An observation checklist focusing on accurate information extraction and vocabulary use can be employed. The final "Fact Sheet" serves as a product for evaluation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their "Marine Ecosystem Fact Sheet" to the class (1-2 minutes per group), or the teacher collects the fact sheets for review. The focus is on whether they successfully outlined the main information from their texts and diagrams.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of outlined information from the provided texts/diagrams.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., "marine," "habitat," "endangered," "conservation," "biodiversity").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and organization of the fact sheet.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of comprehension of descriptive texts and outlining information from charts.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about Panama's ocean life today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading texts and charts about marine conservation is (easy/medium/challenging) for me because..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I use my reading skills to understand the information about ocean life today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about marine animals or conservation graphs do I want to improve next?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading strategies worked well for students when comprehending texts and outlining information from diagrams? Were the reading materials at the appropriate CEFR A2.4 level? How effectively did students use the target vocabulary to describe marine ecosystems? What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with outlining information from charts or synthesizing text details? Were the tasks sufficiently focused on reading comprehension?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction):</b> In the next lesson, students will use the information they read and outlined today to discuss marine conservation and propose actions in pairs and groups, shifting the focus from reading comprehension to active oral communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>